    "narrative": "The Zurich Zebras enter today\u2019s slate with **Yordan Alvarez** standing out as the clear centerpiece of the lineup; his 82.97 score is buoyed by an elite 0.477 xwOBA and a prime matchup against Fedde (4.78 xERA), providing the highest ceiling in our current rotation. He is flanked by a pair of aggressive, high-upside plays in **Trea Turner** (68.68) and **Colt Keith** (68.51). Both benefit from top-of-the-order positioning, and while **Colt Keith** has to contend with the potential for Avila to hold runners, his platoon edge and lineup spot make him an essential play. We are also looking for a significant contribution from **Pete Crow-Armstrong** (61.96), whose elite sprint speed remains our primary weapon to attack the scarcity of the SB category; he faces a tough assignment in Ashcraft, but the platoon advantage and lead-off spot make him a high-efficiency target.\n\nThe margin between a start and the bench was razor-thin for **Josh Smith**, whose 38.43 score fell just short of our 40.0 floor. While **Josh Smith** gained points from a favorable platoon split against Gilbert, his historical BvP struggles and bottom-of-the-order slot ultimately kept him out of the mix. Meanwhile, **Casey Schmitt** (54.63) occupies a vital slot, bolstered by an 11% boost from a dynamic platoon edge and favorable wind conditions that outweigh his pedestrian base projection. We remain cautious with **Kazuma Okamoto** (50.38) and **Gary Sanchez** (53.66); while both have solid matchups, the wind blowing in and the relative skill of Suarez and Freeland respectively cap their output. As we monitor the wire, **Christian Yelich** and **Hyeseong Kim** remain sidelined, leaving our active roster to shoulder the load against several difficult arms like Gausman and Sheehan. Owners should keep a close eye on the weather and confirmation of the lineup for **Gary Sanchez** and **Dillon Dingler**, as any shift in the order could jeopardize the efficiency scores for our catchers.",
